That additional constraint makes the requirement strictly impossible.

The rack and cooling device are inside a sealed room whose walls cannot conduct heat outward. Therefore, the entire 10 kW continuously becomes trapped within the room. Since every allowed component must remain inside the same sealed thermal boundary, no component can provide a continuous net heat sink.

The only possible effects are:

- temporarily distributing the heat within the room;
- temporarily storing it in the device or room contents;
- raising some component above ambient;
- reducing the rack’s power dissipation.

None maintains the rack at ambient temperature indefinitely while consuming 10 kW.

The complete valid portfolio is therefore empty. At least one constraint must change: permit heat export, permit temperature rise, permit finite-duration operation, or reduce continuous power consumption.

# Final Portfolio: No Physically Valid Design

## Explicit contradiction

The rack continuously converts 10 kW of input power into heat.

The complete system is:

- sealed;
- inside a room whose walls cannot conduct heat outward;
- isolated from external heat sinks or reservoirs;
- prohibited from using ventilation, moving fluids, refrigerants, phase-change storage, moving parts, electricity, or colder-than-ambient contact;
- required to keep the rack at ambient temperature indefinitely.

Thus, 10 kW continuously enters the sealed thermal system, while zero net heat can leave it. The system has no permitted mechanism for rejecting or indefinitely storing that energy.

Therefore, the requirements are mutually inconsistent. No physically valid cooling device exists within the stated boundaries.

## Re-anchored boundaries

The valid solution set is:

> **∅ — empty set**

Any proposed solution involving a hidden reservoir, magic material, exothermic reaction, reinterpretation of “continuous,” ambient-temperature radiation, or a new thermodynamic law would violate the explicit boundaries.

At least one boundary must be changed:

- permit heat to leave the room;
- permit the rack or another component to rise above ambient;
- permit finite-duration thermal storage;
- reduce continuous power dissipation below zero net heat rejection;
- or permit an external energy-consuming cooling system.

Without such a change, there is no portfolio to implement.

